---
// Project page for the housing risk map. The map itself is a separate repo (us-housing-map),
// published on this same domain at /us-housing-map/; this page introduces it and links to it.
// Copy: content/housing-page-content.md (final text). Figures are placeholders for now; the real
// ones come in a later PR. Every number in the copy was checked against the us-housing-map repo
// (BACKTEST.md, reports/backtest/secondary.json, reports/regions/).
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlinkCursor from '../../components/BlinkCursor.astro';
import SectionNav from '../../components/SectionNav.astro';

// Site paths, so the links stay on this domain (they work on the live site only, not in
// `npm run dev`, because the map is deployed from its own repo).
const mapUrl = '/us-housing-map/';
const methodologyUrl = '/us-housing-map/methodology.html';
const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/jackrz412/us-housing-map';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Where mortgage stress is building | Jack Roberts"
  description="An interactive county map of US housing burden, and an honest test of whether it
  predicts default."
>
  <p><a href="/">← Home</a></p>
  <SectionNav />

  <!-- Headline, subtitle, links + summary -->
  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow kicker">Housing risk map</p>
    <h1>Where mortgage stress is building<BlinkCursor /></h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      An interactive county map of US housing burden, and an honest test of whether it
      predicts default.
    </p>
    <ul class="links-row" aria-label="Project links">
      <li><a href={mapUrl}>Explore the live map</a></li>
      <li><a href={methodologyUrl}>Methodology</a></li>
      <li><a href={repoUrl}>GitHub repo</a></li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="results-rule under-title" />
    <p class="eyebrow under-title">Summary up front</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        I built an interactive map of every US county, joining Census, CFPB, mortgage-loan and
        Treasury insurance data into measures of how stretched homeowners are.
      </li>
      <li>
        Households on the Pacific coast carry the heaviest payment burden in the country; much of
        the interior buys with the thinnest equity.
      </li>
      <li>
        I tested whether my burden index predicted later delinquency. It didn't, but thin equity
        did, and that points to the next version.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </header>

  <!-- Hero figure (placeholder until the figures PR). The real one links to the live map. -->
  <figure class="hero">
    <a class="placeholder" href={mapUrl}>
      Burden score by county. 1.0 is the benchmark: 2019 national levels, and the 28% rule for
      payments.
    </a>
  </figure>

  <!-- Intro (no heading) -->
  <p>
    It's impossible to under-stress the importance of housing. Like food and water, shelter is what
    lets us stop fighting for survival and start building a life. It's also the largest expense
    most of us will ever take on: housing dictates budgets, where we live, and which jobs we can
    accept. And because homes secure the biggest form of household debt, the mortgage, what happens
    in housing doesn't stay in housing. It reaches banks, retirement accounts and the wider economy,
    as 2008 made clear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Affordability has fallen sharply since 2019: the share of new home loans with debt payments
    above 43% of income rose from 29% to 38% by 2025. I wanted to look past the national
    headlines and
    ask a more personal question: what does a home cost relative to the lives of the people paying
    for it right now? How are mortgage payments fitting into tightening budgets, and how different
    does that look from one county to the next? Are the strains on household finances starting to
    show in the way we buy and pay for homes?
  </p>
  <p>
    Numbers, while precise, are the wrong medium for showing stress across 3,000 places at once.
    Answering these questions was a chance to expand on something I love: visual learning and
    graphics.
  </p>

  <section id="what-the-map-shows">
    <h2>What the map shows</h2>
    <p>
      Both coasts are famous for high housing costs and high-paying jobs, but they don't carry the
      same kind of stress. On the Pacific coast, the strain is the monthly payment: in the
      typical county in California, Oregon and Washington, 37% of mortgaged owners spend 30%
      or more of their income on housing, compared with 28% in the Northeast, and Los Angeles
      reaches 43%. I expected that pattern to
      stretch inland from Colorado, but it doesn't. The Mountain states, Denver included, look much
      like the East Coast.
    </p>
    <p>
      The East has its own pressures. New York City carries the heaviest owner cost burden of any
      major metro I looked at (46% in the typical borough), and the Northeast pays a larger
      share of income in property
      taxes than any other region. Move away from the coasts and a different risk takes over: thin
      equity. In the typical Midwest county, 39% of 2025 conventional purchase loans left the
      buyer with less than 10% equity. In the typical Pacific coast county, only 15% did.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <div class="placeholder">Different regions are stretched in different ways.</div>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Seeing well-known cost-of-living patterns show up in the data was a useful first check that
      these measures capture something real. The more interesting result is that different regions
      are stretched in different ways, and, as the next section shows, those differences matter.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-i-built">
    <h2>What I built</h2>
    <p>
      An interactive map of every US county, with data layers grouped by the role each plays in
      mortgage risk:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Burden:</strong> how much of household income goes to housing, both for existing
        owners and for people who bought in 2025.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Equity:</strong> how many new loans leave buyers with less than 10% equity.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Outcome:</strong> where serious mortgage delinquency sits compared with each
        county's own 2017–2019 normal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Index:</strong> two scores, burden and equity, that combine those measures against
        fixed 2019 benchmarks.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Selecting a county shows every measure side by side, so you can see what's driving its numbers.</p>
    <p>
      Behind the map, a Python pipeline downloads federal data (Census surveys, CFPB delinquency
      data, 2025 mortgage loan records, Treasury insurance premiums), cleans and validates it, and
      writes small data files the page loads directly. A scheduled job reruns the pipeline
      weekly and republishes only when the source data changes; the insurance premiums, a
      one-time Treasury release, were converted to counties once. The map itself is built with
      D3.
    </p>
    <p class="stack">Python · DuckDB · D3 · TopoJSON · GitHub Actions · GitHub Pages</p>
  </section>

  <section id="decisions">
    <h2>Decisions that shaped it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Missing data stays missing.</strong> Counties without reliable data are hatched and
        labeled, not estimated.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Colors mean something.</strong> Shading is tied to fixed benchmarks, like
        the national share of owners paying 30% or more of income on housing, rather than to
        rankings. A
        dark county is over a line, not just in the top seventh.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Payments are calculated loan by loan.</strong> The new-buyer burden comes
        from each loan's actual amount, rate and term, plus county estimates of its property
        taxes and insurance, divided by that borrower's income.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="testing-the-index">
    <h2>Testing the index</h2>
    <p>
      The index is built on the "double trigger" theory of default: households usually default when
      they're stretched <em>and</em> hit by a shock, like a job loss. Before looking at any results,
      I wrote down a test: would counties with higher burden in 2019 see delinquency rise by
      2023–2025?
    </p>
    <p>
      They didn't. Higher-burden counties actually saw delinquency fall slightly more (rank
      correlation −0.16, across 499 counties). The diagnosis: delinquency fell almost everywhere in
      that period, so the test mostly measured how far each county had to fall. And 2019–2025
      never delivered a lasting second trigger. The 2020 unemployment spike was brief,
      forbearance carried borrowers through it, and rising home prices gave owners an equity
      cushion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because that first test mostly measured where each county started, I added one more test,
      specified before running it: compare counties that began at the same delinquency level. The
      combined burden score still showed nothing. But its parts split in two. Thin equity was the
      strongest signal of any measure, followed by lower incomes and the share of applicants denied
      for carrying too much debt. The housing-cost measures, payment-to-income and the share of
      owners paying 30% or more, pointed the opposite way and canceled the others out.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <div class="placeholder">
        Partial rank correlation with 2023–2025 delinquency, controlling for each county's 2017–2019
        level. Bars are 95% bootstrap intervals.
      </div>
    </figure>
    <p>
      That fits the double-trigger theory better than my index did: what mattered was whether
      households had a cushion, not just how large the payment was. The Pacific coast is a good
      example, with the heaviest payment burden in the country and the least thin-equity lending.
      But this came from comparing seven measures after the fact, so I'm treating it as a lead for
      the next version of the index, not a conclusion. Testing that version honestly will take data
      I haven't seen yet.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the map describes where households are stretched today. It doesn't forecast defaults, and
      I'd rather say that plainly than tune the index until it appears to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-could-use-this">
    <h2>Who could use this</h2>
    <p>
      Mortgage brokers and lenders already use area-level data to size markets and focus outreach. A
      county-level view of burden shows where refinance options, first-time buyer programs and
      down-payment assistance would meet the most demand. Housing counselors and local governments
      could use the same view to see where support is likely to be needed before it shows up in
      delinquency numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-i-learned">
    <h2>What I learned</h2>
    <p>
      On the technical side, this was my deepest dive yet into how web graphics are built. An
      interactive map turns out to be many small components, each with one job: a projection that
      places counties on screen, a scale that turns numbers into colors, a data join that matches
      each county to its values. Each is simple on its own; together they make a working map.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bigger lessons were about data. Quality assurance isn't a final step; it's most of the
      work. And combining mismatched sources is where the value is. None of these datasets says much
      on its own: a loan record doesn't know the borrower's taxes, and a Census survey doesn't know
      today's mortgage rates. Joined county by county, they describe something none of them could
      alone.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-i-built-it">
    <h2>How I built it</h2>
    <p>
      Like every project on this site, this one runs on a learning structure I set up deliberately.
      I use Claude Code for bulk programming, so the work moves quickly, while I own the spec,
      review
      every change, and make each design decision. Some things I keep for myself on purpose, like
      all git and version-control work, so I'm building real skills rather than just shipping
      output.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="whats-next">
    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p>
      Shock scenarios: sliders for income, insurance, taxes and rates that show how many counties
      would cross the burden line under each. After that, an AI layer: an MCP server that lets AI
      tools query any county's data and answer questions about it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Links (the copy's repeated links row) -->
  <section id="links">
    <h2>Map, code and data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={mapUrl}>Open the interactive map</a></li>
      <li><a href={methodologyUrl}>Index methodology and backtest</a></li>
      <li><a href={repoUrl}>Source code on GitHub</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The rule and small-caps label under the title, as on the LLM lab page. */
  .results-rule {
    margin: var(--space-7) 0 var(--space-3);
    border: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .results-rule.under-title {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  .eyebrow.under-title {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* The project name above the title, in the eyebrow style. */
  .eyebrow.kicker {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .subtitle {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
  }

  /* Compact links under the subtitle, separated by middle dots. */
  .links-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .links-row li + li::before {
    content: '·';
    margin-right: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  /* The tools behind the map: small, muted, mono. */
  .stack {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  figure {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }

  .hero {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  /* Figure placeholders until the figures PR: final size, the caption text inside. */
  .placeholder {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px dashed var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .hero .placeholder {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
</style>
